.TH AR 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
ar \- create and maintain library archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ar
.RB \-d | \-m | \-p | \-q | \-r | \-t | \-x
.RB [ \-cuv ]
.I archive
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ar
creates and maintains a group of files combined into a single archive
file. Exactly one of the mode letters
.BR d ", " m ", " p ", " q ", " r ", " t ,
or
.B x
must be given, and every option letter (mode plus any modifiers) is
taken from a single option string: the very first command-line
argument, with or without a leading
.BR \- .
No option letter may appear anywhere else on the command line;
.I archive
is always the argument immediately following that option string, and
every argument after that is a
.I file
operand.
.PP
Archives are written in the classic common archive format: an eight-byte
.RI \(lq !<arch>\en \(rq
magic string followed by a sequence of members, each preceded by a
60-byte header (name, modification time, uid, gid, mode, and size, all
as decimal or octal ASCII text, terminated by
.RI \(lq \`\en \(rq ).
Member names longer than 15 bytes are refused at creation time rather
than truncated or extended into a long-name table. This build never
writes a symbol table, and it does not read one either: an archive
written with GNU-style
.RI \(lq // \(rq
long-name members or BSD-style
.RI \(lq #1/len \(rq
extended names is refused with a diagnostic rather than misparsed.
Every archive this utility itself writes uses the plain, inline
15-byte-or-shorter name field, so this limitation never affects an
archive round-tripped through this same
.BR ar .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
Delete the named
.I file
members from
.IR archive .
At least one
.I file
operand is required.
.TP
.B \-m
Not implemented. Relative-positioned moving of members requires
.BR \-a / \-b / \-i ,
which are also not implemented (see below); this build refuses
.B \-m
outright with a diagnostic rather than perform a no-op move.
.TP
.B \-p
Write the contents of the named
.I file
members to standard output, in archive order. With no
.I file
operands, every member is printed.
.TP
.B \-q
Append each
.I file
operand to
.I archive
as a new member, without checking whether a member of the same name
already exists (so a name may end up duplicated).
.TP
.B \-r
Replace each existing member whose name matches a
.I file
operand's basename with that file's current contents, in place, and
append every
.I file
operand that had no existing match to the end of the archive.
Replacing a member never changes its position in the archive.
.TP
.B \-t
Write a table of contents: the name of each member matching a
.I file
operand (or, with no operands, every member), one per line, in
archive order. With
.BR \-v ,
each line instead has the form
\(lq\fImode uid\fR/\fIgid size date name\fR\(rq,
.B ls
\-l\-style (this platform's synthesized uid/gid, both always 0).
.TP
.B \-x
Extract the named
.I file
members (or, with no operands, every member) into the current
directory, restoring each extracted file's permission bits. The
modification time of an extracted file is
.B not
restored from the archive; it is left as the time of extraction.
.TP
.B \-c
Suppress the
\(lq\fIcreating archive\fR\(rq
diagnostic that
.B \-q
and
.B \-r
otherwise print to standard error when
.I archive
does not already exist.
.TP
.B \-u
With
.BR \-r ,
replace an existing member only if the corresponding
.I file
operand's modification time is at least as new as the member's
recorded modification time; an operand that is older than the member
it would otherwise replace is skipped instead.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose: print one line per member as it is deleted, replaced,
appended, extracted, or (with
.BR \-t )
listed.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-b ", " \-i
Not implemented (relative positioning of a new or moved member before
or after a named existing member). Refused with a diagnostic; without
them,
.B \-r
and
.B \-m
always operate at the end of the archive.
.TP
.B \-s
Not implemented. This build never generates or regenerates a symbol
table, so there is nothing for a forced regeneration to do; refused
with a diagnostic rather than silently accepted as a no-op.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-T
Not implemented (refuse-on-collision and allow-truncation policies
for extracting a foreign long-name archive). Refused with a
diagnostic, since this build already refuses at creation time to
write a member name that would not fit, and cannot read a foreign
long-name archive in the first place.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I archive
The pathname of the archive file.
.TP
.I file
A pathname naming a member to operate on (for
.BR \-d ", " \-p ", " \-t ","
and
.BR \-x )
or a file to add or update (for
.BR \-q " and " \-r ).
Only the final pathname component (the basename) is compared against
member names stored in the archive.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An operation-level failure occurred: the archive could not be opened
or was not a valid archive, a member could not be extracted or
written, or a required
.I file
operand could not be read.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error occurred: no mode letter or more than one mode letter
was given, an invalid or unimplemented option was given, or a
required operand was missing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nm (1),
.BR strip (1),
.BR pax (1)
